WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:06.918
Trip distribution connects the trip productions and attractions estimated in the first model step.

2
00:00:06.918 --> 00:00:13.956
It produces an origin-destination pattern: how many trips travel between each pair of zones for each purpose and time period.

3
00:00:13.956 --> 00:00:19.193
The source chapter presents growth-factor methods and gravity models as two major approaches.

4
00:00:19.193 --> 00:00:24.429
Growth-factor methods begin with an observed trip table and expand it to match future totals.

5
00:00:24.429 --> 00:00:33.832
They can be practical when the future resembles the base year, but they tend to preserve existing patterns and perform poorly for new zones or major structural change.

6
00:00:33.832 --> 00:00:40.307
A method that reproduces the past neatly can still miss a future shift in land use, network access, or travel cost.

7
00:00:40.307 --> 00:00:47.402
The gravity model relates interaction to the amount of activity at origins and destinations and to the impedance between them.

8
00:00:47.402 --> 00:00:55.059
In plain language, larger opportunities tend to attract more trips, while greater time, cost, or difficulty tends to reduce interaction.

9
00:00:55.059 --> 00:00:57.480
Friction factors represent that deterrence.

10
00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.647
Different trip purposes can have different distance or time distributions.

11
00:01:01.647 --> 00:01:08.967
Calibration adjusts friction factors and other parameters so modeled trip lengths and interchange patterns resemble observed data.

12
00:01:08.967 --> 00:01:12.232
The analyst should compare more than one regional average.

13
00:01:12.232 --> 00:01:17.300
A model can match mean trip length while missing important corridors or population groups.

14
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:23.212
Validation should examine screenlines, district flows, trip-length distributions, and results by purpose.

15
00:01:23.212 --> 00:01:31.770
The output is conditional on zone boundaries, travel-time skims, land-use totals, and the treatment of trips that begin or end outside the modeled area.

16
00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:35.036
Uncertainty in those inputs carries into every later step.

17
00:01:35.036 --> 00:01:36.301
Torrance application.

18
00:01:36.301 --> 00:01:43.292
This separate local segment cites the City of Torrance Transportation Analysis Requirements for Private Development.

19
00:01:43.292 --> 00:01:54.621
For a hypothetical development study, trip distribution would connect the estimated project trips to destinations and gateways using current evidence and the method accepted for the study.

20
00:01:54.621 --> 00:02:05.287
A reviewer should identify whether the distribution comes from observed turning movements, regional model patterns, nearby comparable uses, market information, or a combination.

21
00:02:05.287 --> 00:02:11.193
The record should state the base year, study period, external gateways, and any manual adjustment.

22
00:02:11.193 --> 00:02:18.304
It should also explain how construction, remote work, unusual count days, or a changed network affect transferability.

23
00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:30.115
The model geography matters in Torrance because travel crosses municipal boundaries and because employment, commercial, industrial, residential, and freight activities generate different patterns.

24
00:02:30.115 --> 00:02:36.924
A coarse regional zone may need local refinement, but local detail should remain consistent with regional totals.

25
00:02:36.924 --> 00:02:44.095
Sensitivity testing could compare plausible destination patterns rather than hiding uncertainty in a single percentage.

26
00:02:44.095 --> 00:02:53.134
The analyst should keep this circulation question distinct from the separate environmental vehicle-miles-traveled analysis described in City guidance.

27
00:02:53.134 --> 00:02:56.991
No origin-destination result or project finding is offered here.

28
00:02:56.991 --> 00:03:02.535
Current City requirements, observed data, and project-specific direction would be necessary.

29
00:03:02.535 --> 00:03:08.863
The application demonstrates how to expose the evidence and assumptions behind a trip-distribution table.

30
